WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.044 --> 00:00:00.960
(notification whistles)

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:04.950
- [Reporter] This is why Twitter
has $13 billion in new debt

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:07.533
that it took on at the end of 2022.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.210
Elon Musk didn't start Twitter's problems.

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:16.560
It hadn't booked an
annual profit since 2019,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.510
but in the months after that tweet,

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.640
its finances have drained.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.320
Revenue fell an estimated 40%
year over year in December.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.020
Still, Musk is projecting optimism.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.130
- [Musk] I think we've got a shot

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.050
at being cash flow positive next quarter.

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.990
- [Reporter] But just
how possible is that?

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.970
Let's look at Twitter's cash flow before

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.900
and after Musk's takeover to
see how its revenue has changed

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.623
and why he insists Twitter
will take flight again soon.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.340
Twitter's cash flow problems started

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.740
before Musk's takeover of the company.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.430
It had posted a loss in
eight of the last 10 years.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:01:00.780
- The last full year that
we have figures for is 2021.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.440
They booked about 5 billion in revenue

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.020
and also booked about
5.5 billion in costs,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.570
which goes to show that
the company's finances

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.230
even before the takeover
were a little stressed.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.240
- [Reporter] But for
the money it was making,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.970
it relied heavily on advertising.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.686
Take a look at the second quarter of 2022.

00:01:20.686 --> 00:01:22.770
Advertising accounted for more

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.993
than 90% of Twitter's revenue.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.704
Fast forward to October

00:01:27.704 --> 00:01:31.623
when Elon Musk purchased
Twitter for $44 billion.

00:01:32.761 --> 00:01:33.750
(Musk shouts)

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.946
That buy saddled the company
with that $13 billion in debt.

00:01:37.946 --> 00:01:40.740
Analysts estimated at the time
that this would put Twitter

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.780
on the hook for annual interest payments

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:47.780
of more than $1 billion
compared to 51 million in 2021.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.540
- The debt is going to drain
the company's resources.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:56.520
He added a huge burden to
the company's finances.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.160
- [Reporter] Musk came in
with big ideas for Twitter,

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:02.160
including cutting down its
reliance on advertising revenue

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.010
and focusing on subscription services.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.380
But one idea that he focused heavily on

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:11.490
was promoting what he
refers to as free speech.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.186
Musk began reinstating accounts

00:02:13.186 --> 00:02:16.320
that had been banned for
tweets that violated policies

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.480
and limiting content moderation.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.900
- Twitter has become a little
bit more of a chaotic platform

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.060
and place since Elon took over.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.490
- [Reporter] Last year saw a
slowdown in online marketing

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.410
that affected many platforms

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.750
due to people spending less time online

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:33.120
and broader economic concerns.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.460
But Twitter had additional issues

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.160
because some of Musk's
changes didn't sit well

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.380
with some big advertisers.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:43.813
Within weeks of the takeover,
companies including Chipotle

00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:46.260
and United Airlines had pulled back

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.690
or paused their ad
spending on the platform,

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:51.420
hurting Twitter's primary
source of revenue.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.300
- Advertisers are really
focused on brand safety

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.000
so that when their ads appear online

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.400
or anywhere for that matter,
they want want to know

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.800
that the content that it's surrounding

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.450
is not offensive or not inflammatory.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.740
- [Reporter] Musk has said Twitter

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.740
must be a respectable
platform for advertisers,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.980
and Twitter says brand
safety is a priority.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:15.660
While advertisers fled,

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.270
Musk was taking stock of his new company.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.770
He said in early November

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.920
that Twitter was losing $4 million a day.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.450
- Because I'm just thinking
about like, how do we...

00:03:26.947 --> 00:03:29.190
I'm just like, you know,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.160
just get Twitter to be in a
financially healthy place.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.080
- The main way that's being addressed,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.780
we're told, is through cost cutting.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.330
- [Reporter] Twitter went
from about 8,000 employees

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:42.810
before the sale to
around 2,000 in December,

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:45.540
but these job cuts have
come with their own price.

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:49.350
- When you fire whole teams
and take them off the site,

00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:52.170
suddenly there are
people who had expertise

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.240
in running a very complicated machine

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.290
that are not around anymore,
and it's not a surprise

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.470
that there have been
unexpected unplanned outages.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.260
- [Reporter] Twitter hasn't
said much about recent outages,

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.370
but the company responded

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.732
to a request for comment with this.

00:04:07.732 --> 00:04:08.850
(message beeps)

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:10.530
Aside from cost cutting,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.960
Twitter has made moves to grow revenue.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.633
- Please subscribe to Twitter
Verified, eight bucks,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:21.480
and actually it's technically,
it's 7.99, so slightly less.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:23.220
- [Reporter] In December, it relaunched

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.130
its paid subscription
service Twitter Blue.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.130
As of March, it's estimated
that the subscription service

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:33.270
has about 450,000 users
according to Travis Brown,

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.570
a software developer who
tracks social media platforms.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:40.560
Now Twitter Blue is $11
for mobile phone users.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.340
- We haven't seen a big
uptake of Twitter Blue yet.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.520
There aren't enough incentives yet

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.600
or reasons to join Twitter
Blue to cause a mass signup.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.090
- [Reporter] So Musk is also
appealing to advertisers

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.890
to come back to the platform.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.320
- [Musk] What I'd say to
advertisers and brands is,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:00.360
you know, use Twitter yourself

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.640
and believe what you see on Twitter

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:04.860
not what you read in the newspapers

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.810
'cause what you see on
Twitter is the real thing

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.970
and what you read in newspapers is not.

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.430
- [Reporter] Currently, Musk
says the company earns five

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.790
to six cents an hour spent
by its users on the platform,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.983
an amount Musk isn't satisfied with.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:20.490
Still, he sees potential saying...

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:22.920
- [Musk] There will be a
massive increase in revenue

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.720
because it is now useful.

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.530
- He sincerely believes that
through targeted advertising

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:33.120
more specific forms of content
instead of just general ads

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.770
for the public writ large,

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.840
advertisers will spend
more because they feel

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:38.970
like they're reaching their
target audience on Twitter.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:40.590
- [Reporter] But are these efforts enough

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.810
to actually make that happen?

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.990
One analyst says the company
could at least break even

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:49.500
in nine months based on
estimates of recent cost cuts

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.530
and stabilizing ad revenue.

00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:54.810
Twitter could also negotiate
down its interest payments,

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:58.140
and Musk could contribute
more of his personal wealth.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.970
But whether the company books a profit

00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.957
depends on what direction Musk takes.

00:06:02.957 --> 00:06:06.090
- He wants to turn it into
like an everything platform

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.667
where you can essentially
integrate commercial activity

00:06:09.667 --> 00:06:12.555
with reading the news,
with talking to friends.

00:06:12.555 --> 00:06:15.090
And I think that's the long-term vision,

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.160
but first he's gotta be
sure that the company

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.583
can pay its bills, and that
is still a bit in question.

00:06:21.712 --> 00:06:25.212
(soft instrumental music)

